WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.920
When Darwin traveled to the Galapagos,
he saw finches with beaks of different

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:09.750
shapes and sizes, exquisitely adapted
to feed from differently shaped flowers.

3
00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:15.850
From these observations, he established
the theory of evolution. What is

4
00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:21.780
evolution? Let's explore this concept
with an example. How did animals adapt

5
00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:27.230
their eyesight when they left water to
live on dry land? Tiktaalik is thought

6
00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:31.160
to represent the evolutionary
transition from fish to amphibians,

7
00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:37.110
about 375 million years ago. As we know
from swimming underwater and opening

8
00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:41.510
our own eyes, images are quite blurry.
Humans have not evolved to see well

9
00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:46.310
underwater. The same is true for
aquatic animals. They can't see well

10
00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.780
with the eye-air interface on dry land.
Without good eyesight, protecting

11
00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.730
oneself and one's family, attracting a
mate, and finding food are difficult

12
00:00:56.730 --> 00:01:01.260
and life threatening. These
difficulties are called selective

13
00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.150
pressures, and represent the driving
force of evolution.

14
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:10.770
How do we go from a water dwelling
animal to one that can see on land?

15
00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:16.520
First, random genetic mutations and DNA
create diversity in the shapes and

16
00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:21.930
properties of eyes. These variations
may be beneficial, neutral or

17
00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:25.450
detrimental. The beneficial ones are
key.

18
00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:31.080
The ancestors of Tiktaalik, with
improved eyesight on land, have access

19
00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:35.600
to a tremendous bounty of food. They
grow more rapidly and have more

20
00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.590
offspring that all bear the mutation
with improved eyesight. At each

21
00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:44.880
generation, more genetic diversity
arises, and the selective pressure of

22
00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.010
finding mates, avoiding disasters, and
getting to good food favors the fittest,

23
00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:55.660
that is the Tiktaaliks with better
eyesight. In this way, the eyesight on

24
00:01:55.660 --> 00:02:01.040
land gets better and better. This is
evolution. A new population has evolved

25
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.340
with a well adapted and improved trait;
the fittest survive.

